text:
The weekly rhythm


Show the R code
biplot(W, center = TRUE, scaled = FALSE) |&gt;
  PCA(group.aes = weekday$state) |&gt;
  samples(col = state_cols, pch = 16) |&gt;
  alpha.bags(col = state_cols) |&gt;
  axes(col = "grey35", label.cex = 0.75, ticks = 5) |&gt;
  plot()





PCA biplot of the weekday panel. Each point is one store department, coloured by state, and each shaded region is an alpha bag enclosing the central 95 percent of its state’s points. The seven axes are the days of the week, calibrated in log ratios to the store department’s own average day.




This two-dimensional picture reproduces 92 percent of the variation in the seven-column table, so it is a faithful summary.
Read the axes first. Saturday and Sunday point to the left and Monday to Thursday point to the right, with Friday pointing down between them. The tick marks on the Saturday axis reach 0.4 and beyond; a log ratio of 0.4 is about 50 percent above the store department’s average day. On the Tuesday to Thursday side the ticks are negative. So every store department sells more on the weekend, which is no surprise. The biplot is centred, so what the spread of points shows is how each store department departs from that shared pattern.
The states do not have the same weekend. The Wisconsin bag sits low in the picture, pulled along the Saturday and Friday axes. The California bag stretches up and to the left, along the Sunday axis, and Texas sits between them. Projecting the points onto the axes confirms it: in Wisconsin the Friday lift is positive and the Sunday lift is less than half of California’s, while California’s peak day is Sunday. In plain terms, Wisconsin’s weekend shopping starts on Friday and peaks on Saturday, and California’s peaks on Sunday. Texas is in between.


Show the R code
biplot(W, center = TRUE, scaled = FALSE) |&gt;
  PCA(group.aes = weekday$dept_id) |&gt;
  samples(pch = 16) |&gt;
  alpha.bags() |&gt;
  axes(col = "grey35", label.cex = 0.75, ticks = 5) |&gt;
  plot()





The same weekday biplot, now with the points coloured by department and one alpha bag per department.




Departments differ less than states do. Colouring the same points by department gives bags that overlap much more, which says that where a department is matters more for its weekly rhythm than what it sells. Still, two departments stand out. HOBBIES_2 (magenta) hugs the right side of the picture near the weekday axes: its weekend lift is the smallest of any department, only around 10 percent above its average day. HOUSEHOLD_1 (black) reaches furthest to the left: household goods have the biggest weekend swing, with Saturdays around 25 percent above average and midweek days around 15 percent below. FOODS_2 (green) sits highest in the picture because its lift carries over into Monday, the only department for which Monday is an above-average day.

The yearly rhythm


Show the R code
biplot(M, center = TRUE, scaled = FALSE) |&gt;
  PCA(group.aes = month$dept_id) |&gt;
  samples(pch = 16) |&gt;
  alpha.bags() |&gt;
  axes(col = "grey35", label.cex = 0.75, ticks = 5) |&gt;
  plot()





PCA biplot of the month panel. Points are the same 70 store departments, coloured by department with an alpha bag per department. The twelve axes are the months of the year, again calibrated in log ratios to the store department’s own average day.




The month picture explains 75 percent of the variation, less than the weekday picture, and one glance shows why: most of that variation belongs to a single department.
One department has a season. The HOBBIES_2 bag sits far to the left, alone, in the direction of the October, November and December axes and directly away from June and July. Reading off the calibrations, an average HOBBIES_2 department sells about 40 percent more per day in October than in a typical month, and about 20 percent more in December, but roughly 20 to 25 percent less in June and July. The other six departments crowd together near the origin, which means their sales barely change from month to month. Whatever HOBBIES_2 contains, it is the seasonal, run-up-to-the-holidays part of the shop.
Not every month is worth an axis. biplotEZ reports how well each axis is represented in the picture. October scores 0.98 and June to August all score above 0.85, so the picture reproduces those months well. May scores 0.01: the May axis is drawn but is essentially useless, because May is the one month in which nothing distinguishes one store department from another.
A few individual outliers. Away from the HOBBIES_2 cluster, the point that strays furthest is FOODS_2 in store WI_1, up and to the left, away from the June to August axes and towards January. It sells about 20 percent more in January and 20 percent less in June to August than its average, a winter pattern that none of the other food departments share. TX_3’s FOODS_1 department shows a milder version of the same thing.


Show the R code
biplot(M, center = TRUE, scaled = FALSE) |&gt;
  PCA(group.aes = month$state) |&gt;
  samples(col = state_cols, pch = 16) |&gt;
  alpha.bags(col = state_cols) |&gt;
  axes(col = "grey35", label.cex = 0.75, ticks = 5) |&gt;
  plot()





The month biplot coloured by state.




Geography barely matters over the year. Coloured by state, the three bags lie almost on top of one another. Wisconsin’s bag reaches a little further towards February and March and a little less towards July, so its stores are slightly quieter in summer, but this is a small effect next to the weekday differences. The yearly rhythm is set by the goods on the shelf, and the weekly rhythm by where the shelf stands.

